Key words: Ore prospecting; Covered area; Gas geochemistry; PMGRA;
Weilasituo Sn polymetallic deposit; Zhugongtang Pb-Zn deposit
Journal Pre-proof

During solid mineral explorations, most ores are composed of metal sulfides, co-exist or are accompanied by metal sulfides. Previous research results revealed that organic matter participated in the mineralization process of various kinds of ore deposits, and the contents of hydrocarbon components were high in ores (Zhang et al., 2012). Therefore, $H_{2}S$ , $SO_{2}$ , $CH_{4}$ , and $CO_{2}$ can all be regarded as direct and effective indices (Hinkle et al., 1978; McCarthy, 1986; Oakes et al., 1987; Reid et al., 1990;
Journal Pre-proof

Fridman., 1990; Zhang et al., 2016). In contrast to other gases in the natural world, these gases exhibit the following characteristics: (1) strong penetrability. Driven by high temperature and pressure under the ground, they have extremely strong penetrability and volatility, and this is also the foundation of gas measurement; (2) they have vertically upward migration. The vertical motion of gas exceeds horizontal motion. Accordingly, blind ore deposits and deep deposits under the ground can be detected and hydrocarbon distribution ranges can be determined; and (3) the gas concentration is very low. Gas concentrations drop steadily during the migration from underground deep to the surface, thereby setting high requirements for instrumental sensitivity. On that basis, our team developed a Portable Multi-component Gas Rapid Analyzer (PMGRA) that can simultaneously measure concentrations of $\mathrm{H}_2\mathrm{S}$ , $\mathrm{SO}_2$ , $\mathrm{CH}_4$ , and $\mathrm{CO}_2$ . Moreover, the analyzer, with fast analytical speed, high precision, low cost, portability, environmental friendliness and easy promotion, is capable of measuring various mineralization-related gases at the same time.

